<<<
= resembling in this respect.
===
+ = resembling in this respect.
>>>

<<<
The Committee was today, like yesterday, composed of the Pollon genilemen.
===
*The Committee was* today, like yesterday, *composed of the following gentlemen.*
>>>

<<<
The Turks would appreciate the change, as, unlike Koweit, their political title is here beyond dispute.
===
*The Turks would appreciate the change, as,* unlike Koweit, *their political title is here beyond dispute.*
>>>

<<<
It is certain that now, unlike the closing years of last century, quotation from his poetry is singularly rare.
===
*It is certain that* now, unlike the closing years *of last century, quotation from his poetry is singularly rare.*
>>>

<<<
We may have 110, like last year, when Paignton . . Jersey all enjoyed a sun-bath of nearly 200 hours.
===
*We may have 110,* like last year, *when Paignton . . & Jersey all enjoyed a sun-bath of nearly 200 hours.*
>>>

<<<
governed by like must be a noun, not an adverb
===
governed by *like* must be a noun, not an adverb
>>>

<<<
Yesterday & last year are not nouns, but an adverb & an adverbial phrase; & Koweit & the closing years, meaning at Koweit & in the closing years, have also only a deceitful appearance of being nouns.
===
*Yesterday* & *last year* are not nouns, but an adverb & an adverbial phrase; & *Koweit* & *the closing years*, meaning *at* Koweit & *in* the closing years, have also only a deceitful appearance of being nouns.
>>>

<<<
People get alarmed on each occasion on which (like the present case) dying children suddenly appear.
===
*People get alarmed on each occasion* on which (like the present case) *dying children suddenly appear.*
>>>

<<<
He has completed a new work in which, like its author’s recent books, no failing in sparkle or vigour will be traceable.
===
*He has completed a new work* in which, like its author’s recent books, *no failing in sparkle or vigour will be traceable.*
>>>

<<<
And then came the war; like many another English village, it filtered slowly, very slowly, through to his.
===
*And then came the war*; like many another English village, *it filtered slowly, very slowly, through* to his.
>>>

<<<
the preceding noun to which like is attached must be
===
the preceding noun to which *like* is attached must be
>>>

<<<
The preceding nouns are which (i.e. occasion), which (i.e. work), & his (i.e. village), governed by
===
The preceding nouns are *which* (i.e. occasion), *which* (i.e. work), & *his* (i.e. village), governed by
>>>

<<<
governed by on, in, & to; instead of like, read as in the present case, as in its author’s recent books, & as to many another.
===
governed by *on*, *in*, & *to*; instead of *like*, read *as in the present case, as in its author’s recent books, & as to many another.*
>>>

<<<
his private life was projligate; like Antony, he was an insatiate gambler.
===
*his private* life was *profligate; like Antony, he was an insatiate gambler.*
>>>

<<<
/When the raging tearing propaganda was started, it was thought that, like Mr Balfour, his convictions on the fiscal question were unsettled, The limitation is that the ~ &’
===
/*When the raging tearing propaganda was started, it was thought that,* like Mr Balfour, *his* convictions *on the fiscal question were unsettled.* The limitation is that the
>>>

<<<
+ word governed by like must be in pari materia with the one to which it is compared.
===
+ word governed by *like* must be *in pari materia* with the one to which it is compared.
>>>

<<<
The predecessor & Mr Balfour are not so related to life & convictions; but Antony is to he, & that sentence alone will pass muster.
===
The *predecessor* & *Mr Balfour* are not so related to *life* & *convictions*; but *Antony* is to *he*, & that sentence alone will pass muster.
>>>

<<<
This mistake, however, of | comparing unlike things is not, like | the others, peculiar to like, but is a slovenly parsimony of words that may occur in many other constructions.
===
This mistake, however, of comparing unlike things is not, like the others, peculiar to *like*, but is a slovenly parsimony of words that may occur in many other constructions.
>>>

<<<
-like. ‘In formations
===
**-like.** ‘In formations
>>>

<<<
nouns in -/ require the hyphen
===
nouns in *-l* require the hyphen
>>>

<<<
cowl-like, eellike, flail-like
===
cowl-like, eel-like, flail-like
>>>

<<<
like, v. 1. L. makes likable; see MUTE E.
===
**like, v. 1.** L. makes *likable*; see ^^MUTE E.^^
>>>

<<<
2. I would l. Even on those who use should & would idiomatically
===
**2.** *I would l.* Even on those who use *should* & *would* idiomatically
>>>

<<<
under all ordinary temptations the verb J. seems to exercise a corrupting influence; a couple of examples follow pro formå, but anyone can find as many as he pleases with very little search:—We would l. to ask one or two questions on our own account.
===
under all ordinary temptations the verb *l.* seems to exercise a corrupting influence; a couple of examples follow *pro formâ*, but anyone can find as many as he pleases with very little search:—*We would l. to ask one or two questions on our own account.*
>>>

<<<
/There is one paragraph in it that I would l. to refer to. There is indeed
===
/*There is one paragraph in it that I would l. to refer to.* There is indeed
>>>

<<<
if the thing had to be said without the use of the verb like, would & not should is the form to use: We would ask, that I would refer to; but that has nothing to do with what is right when the verb like is used.
===
if the thing had to be said without the use of the verb *like, would* & not *should* is the form to use: *We would ask, that I would refer to*; but that has nothing to do with what is right when the verb *like* is used.
>>>

<<<
(When the post came I would like to be allowed to carry it in,=1I used to like), I would l. is no better
===
(*When the post came I would like to be allowed to carry it in,* =I used to like), *I would l.* is no better
>>>

<<<
than any of the wills & woulds that are weil recognized as Scotch, Irish, American, & other kinds of English,
===
than any of the *wills* & *woulds* that are well recognized as Scotch, Irish, American, & other kinds of English,
>>>

<<<
but not English English. If the SHALL & WILL idiom is worth preserving at all, I would l. is wrong, & I should i. right.
===
but not English English. If the ^^SHALL^^ & ^^WILL^^ idiom is worth preserving at all, *I would l.* is wrong, & *I should l.* right.
>>>
